 about how we could improve this code. First, let us consider what exactly the `MinPool2d_x` class does. It takes an input tensor `x`, applies a max pooling operation to it, and then negates the result. This means that the output of the min pooling layer will be the negative of the maximum value in each window. We can use this information to our advantage when designing the new network architecture.

Now, let us look at the `EVE` class. As mentioned earlier, it consists of two convolutional layers followed by a batch normalization layer. However, instead of using just one type of pooling operation (max pooling), we want to combine both types of pooling operations into a single layer. To do so, we need to modify the `forward()` method slightly. Instead of concatenating the outputs of the max and min pooling layers, we will add them together element-wise. Then, we will pass the resulting tensor through a convolutional layer and a batch normalization layer.

To implement this idea, we first need to create a new class called `CombinedPoolingLayer`. This class should inherit from `nn.Module` and contain a single forward() method that performs the desired combination of pooling operations. Here is a possible implementation:
